              "We consider the problem of operating a multi-tenant container platform in which the entire compute estate—a single, GPU-rich node with one large local disk—resides behind carrier-grade NAT, reachable from the public Internet primarily through a rented cloud relay. This topology is increasingly common at the edge: educational and small-team platforms, laboratory clusters, and regional AI inference sites all share the shape of modest capital expenditure, no managed Kubernetes control plane, and ingress constrained to whatever tunnels can be established to an inexpensive virtual machine. Tenants are provisioned as groups, each receiving a dedicated pod, a persistent home directory, and optional GPU access. The platform must therefore solve, with essentially no dedicated infrastructure budget, the same problems large clusters solve with ingress controllers, volume provisioners, autoscalers, and dedicated schedulers.",
