              "The actuator is Kubernetes in-place pod resize: a patch against the pod's resize subresource that adjusts requests on a live pod without restart, available on recent cluster versions. Raw actuation on every poll would thrash, because near-idle pods exhibit cgroup CPU noise of tens of millicores that once produced several patches per minute, each patch a write to the API server and a cgroup adjustment by the kubelet. The controller therefore requires two independent conditions to fire on the same dimension: relative drift exceeding ten percent and an absolute deadband of fifty millicores for CPU or sixty-four mebibytes for memory. Either condition alone is insufficient—relative drift alone fires on noise near zero, where ten percent of almost nothing is still almost nothing, and absolute drift alone fires on proportionally trivial changes for large pods, where fifty millicores is measurement error. The conjunction implements a noise floor that scales with the workload, and a per-group cooldown plus a warm-up period of several samples after pod creation further suppress transients that no deadband can catch, such as the startup burst of a model-loading process.",
              "In-place resize covers only live pods. When a group has no running pod, the controller strategically patches the Deployment template so that an involuntary recreation—node reboot, eviction, crash—cannot resurrect stale over-reservations from months-old observations. The patch type is load-bearing: a strategic-merge patch updates the resources map within the existing container entry in place, whereas a JSON merge patch replaces the entire containers list and silently drops fields such as the image, producing a Deployment that recreates pods with no container image at all—a failure that surfaces only at the next rollout, far from its cause. The reconciliation policy is deliberately asymmetric: the automatic loop only lowers requests, treating reduction as low-risk because an under-reservation degrades throughput rather than correctness, while raises occur synchronously when the user resizes or restarts a workload, keeping the control loop from racing tenant intent and from being blamed for a capacity reduction the tenant did not request.",
